sections.proposed_first_experiments:
- {'what': 'Run 6-10 small open models over a 500-item labeled benchmark slice and record per-item correctness for each model.', 'measure': 'Per-item failure count (0 to N models wrong) and the fraction of items failed by ALL models.', 'expected_outcome': 'A non-trivial set (a few percent) of items where every model fails, far above what independent error rates would predict.'}
- {'what': 'Hand-code the universal-failure items into 5-8 blind-spot categories with two annotators and reconcile disagreements.', 'measure': "Category distribution and inter-annotator agreement (e.g. Cohen's kappa) on the taxonomy.", 'expected_outcome': 'Most universal failures fall into a small number of recurring categories (e.g. tokenization/arithmetic, stale facts), demonstrating structured shared blind spots.'}
- {'what': 'Build a null model assuming independent per-model error and compute the expected number of all-fail items, comparing to observed.', 'measure': 'Observed vs expected count of all-models-wrong items; an over-representation ratio.', 'expected_outcome': 'Observed universal failures substantially exceed the independence prediction, confirming correlated rather than random blind spots.'}

- {'what': 'Generate 4-bit, 8-bit, and 2-3 LoRA variants of one base model and run all of them plus an unrelated same-size base over a 500-item labeled benchmark.', 'measure': 'Pairwise exact-answer agreement and error-overlap (Jaccard on wrong items) within the derived family vs vs the unrelated base.', 'expected_outcome': 'Derived variants agree with each other and the base far more than with the unrelated model, showing derivation barely decorrelates.'}
- {'what': "On an open-ended prompt set, embed each variant's responses and compute pairwise semantic similarity.", 'measure': 'Mean cosine similarity of response embeddings within the family vs across to the unrelated base.', 'expected_outcome': 'Within-family semantic similarity is high; quantization and light LoRA produce only marginal divergence.'}
- {'what': "Quantify residual homogeneity as the fraction of the base model's specific wrong answers reproduced by each derivative.", 'measure': "Per-derivative reproduction rate of the base's exact wrong answers.", 'expected_outcome': 'A large majority of base errors persist unchanged through quantization and light fine-tuning.'}

- {'what': 'Measure baseline inter-model error overlap on a 500-item labeled benchmark with neutral prompts across 4-6 models.', 'measure': 'Mean pairwise error-overlap (Jaccard) and exact-answer agreement.', 'expected_outcome': 'High baseline agreement/error overlap establishing the monoculture baseline.'}
- {'what': 'Re-run all models under 3-4 distinct persona system prompts and recompute inter-model homogeneity.', 'measure': 'Change in pairwise agreement and error overlap relative to baseline.', 'expected_outcome': 'Persona conditioning modestly reduces correlation, but a substantial shared core of agreement persists.'}
- {'what': 'Track benchmark accuracy per persona to test the quality-preservation constraint.', 'measure': 'Accuracy delta per persona vs neutral baseline (success criterion: diversity gains with <2-3 point accuracy loss).', 'expected_outcome': 'Some personas preserve accuracy while adding diversity; others trade quality for divergence, mapping the cost curve.'}

- {'what': 'Collect per-model correctness vectors for 8-12 models on a 500-item labeled benchmark and build the pairwise error-correlation matrix.', 'measure': 'Mean pairwise error correlation rho_bar and the resulting effective-N estimate.', 'expected_outcome': 'rho_bar is clearly positive, yielding an effective-N substantially below the raw model count (e.g. 8 models behaving like 3-4 independent ones).'}
- {'what': 'Simulate majority-vote ensembles of size 1..N by sampling model subsets and measure accuracy.', 'measure': 'Ensemble accuracy as a function of ensemble size (diminishing-returns curve).', 'expected_outcome': 'Accuracy plateaus well before all models are added, consistent with the low effective-N.'}
- {'what': 'Compare the empirical saturation point of the curve against the analytic effective-N.', 'measure': 'Agreement between where the curve flattens and the effective-N value.', 'expected_outcome': 'The saturation point roughly matches effective-N, validating it as a cheap predictor of useful ensemble size.'}

- {'what': 'Measure baseline inter-model response similarity on 200 clean prompts across 5-8 models.', 'measure': 'Mean pairwise cosine similarity of response embeddings.', 'expected_outcome': 'A moderate-to-high baseline homogeneity level to compare against.'}
- {'what': 'Apply 4-5 perturbation types to each prompt and re-measure inter-model similarity on the perturbed versions.', 'measure': 'Change in pairwise response similarity (perturbed minus clean) per perturbation type.', 'expected_outcome': 'Some perturbations (e.g. adversarial prefixes) increase homogeneity as models collapse to shared refusal/degenerate templates, while others increase divergence.'}
- {'what': "On a labeled subset, track whether models' wrong answers overlap more under perturbation than on clean items.", 'measure': 'Error-overlap (Jaccard on wrong items) clean vs perturbed.', 'expected_outcome': 'Correlated error overlap rises under stress for at least some perturbation classes, evidencing shared OOD failure modes.'}

- {'what': 'Run base and instruct checkpoints of 3-4 families on a 300-item multiple-choice benchmark and compute within-group inter-model agreement for each group.', 'measure': 'Mean pairwise answer-agreement among base models vs among instruct models.', 'expected_outcome': 'Instruct models agree with each other noticeably more than base models do, suggesting alignment increases homogeneity.'}
- {'what': 'On a set of open-ended prompts, embed responses and compute within-group response similarity for base vs instruct groups.', 'measure': 'Mean pairwise response-embedding cosine similarity per group.', 'expected_outcome': 'Instruct responses are markedly more similar across models than base completions.'}
- {'what': 'Compare error overlap on the labeled items between the two groups to see if alignment specifically correlates the mistakes.', 'measure': 'Error-overlap (Jaccard on wrong items) base group vs instruct group.', 'expected_outcome': 'Instruct group shows higher correlated-error overlap, pinpointing alignment as a homogenizing force.'}

- {'what': "Have 5-8 open judges score a few hundred Infinity-Chat response pairs and compute each judge's per-item deviation from the human-preference distribution.", 'measure': 'Per-judge deviation from the human consensus and the pairwise correlation matrix of those deviations.', 'expected_outcome': 'Judge deviations are positively correlated, indicating shared rather than independent bias.'}
- {'what': "Construct an independence null (shuffle judges' deviations across items) and compare correlated-error magnitude to observed.", 'measure': 'Observed mean inter-judge deviation correlation vs the null distribution.', 'expected_outcome': 'Observed correlation significantly exceeds the null, confirming a shared judge bias.'}
- {'what': 'Test whether a majority/mean ensemble of judges is closer to human preference than a single judge.', 'measure': 'Deviation-from-human of the judge ensemble vs the best single judge.', 'expected_outcome': 'Ensembling reduces noise but leaves a persistent shared-bias floor, showing limited robustness gains.'}

- {'what': 'Query 8-10 models on ~300 hallucination-eliciting items with ground truth and record every wrong answer.', 'measure': 'Per-item count of models wrong and the distribution of distinct wrong answers per item.', 'expected_outcome': 'On many items the wrong answers cluster onto a single shared false fact rather than scattering.'}
- {'what': 'Compute the same-wrong-answer rate across model pairs and compare to a random-hallucination independence baseline.', 'measure': 'Observed fraction of model pairs giving the identical wrong answer vs the baseline-expected fraction.', 'expected_outcome': 'Observed shared-hallucination rate substantially exceeds the independence baseline.'}
- {'what': 'Evaluate a consensus verifier: accept an answer when >=k models agree, and measure how often this rubber-stamps a shared hallucination.', 'measure': 'False-acceptance rate of the agreement heuristic on hallucination items.', 'expected_outcome': "Agreement-based verification accepts a non-trivial number of correlated hallucinations, demonstrating the heuristic's blind spot."}

- {'what': "Elicit verbalized and logit confidence from 5-8 models on a 300-item labeled QA set and tag each model's high-confidence-wrong items.", 'measure': 'Per-model count and identity of high-confidence-wrong items.', 'expected_outcome': 'Each model has a substantial set of confidently-wrong items, and the sets are not disjoint.'}
- {'what': 'Compute pairwise overlap (Jaccard) of high-confidence-wrong items across models and compare to a chance baseline.', 'measure': 'Observed mean pairwise overlap vs the independence-baseline overlap.', 'expected_outcome': 'Observed overlap significantly exceeds chance, indicating shared confident-error items.'}
- {'what': 'Simulate a confidence-thresholded abstention rule and measure how often all models would simultaneously fail to abstain on a shared wrong item.', 'measure': 'Rate of correlated non-abstention on confidently-wrong items across the model pool.', 'expected_outcome': "A non-trivial set of items where every model is confidently wrong and none would abstain, exposing the control's correlated failure."}

- {'what': 'Run the self-correction probe across 5-8 models on ~300 labeled items with injected erroneous answers and record per-item correction success.', 'measure': 'Per-model correction-failure set (items the model fails to fix).', 'expected_outcome': 'Each model fails to correct a meaningful fraction, and the failure sets are not disjoint.'}
- {'what': 'Compute pairwise overlap of failed-to-correct items across models and compare to an independence baseline.', 'measure': 'Observed mean overlap (Jaccard) of uncorrectable items vs baseline-expected overlap.', 'expected_outcome': 'Observed overlap exceeds the baseline, evidencing a shared self-correction blind spot.'}
- {'what': 'Identify the items that NO model can self-correct and qualitatively characterize them.', 'measure': 'Count and category description of universally-uncorrectable items.', 'expected_outcome': 'A small, structured set of error types that the entire model population fails to self-correct.'}
